      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we can express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\) and then eliminate \\(t\\) to find a relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\\[x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t},\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}.\\]\n\nNext, we add and subtract these two equations to eliminate \\(t\\):\n\\[x + y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) + \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = 2,\\]\n\\[x - y = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right) - \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = \\frac{2}{t}.\\]\n\nNow, we have the system of equations:\n\\[x + y = 2,\\]\n\\[x - y = \\frac{2}{t}.\\]\n\nAdding these two equations, we get:\n\\[2x = 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\implies x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}.\\]\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get:\n\\[2y = 2 - \\frac{2}{t} \\implies y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}.\\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can see that \\(x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\) and \\(y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\). Multiplying these two equations together, we get:\n\\[xy = \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\right) = 1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2 = 1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}.\\]\n\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\\[xy = 1 - \\frac{1}{t^2} \\implies \\frac{xy + \\frac{1}{t^2}}{t^2} = 1 \\implies \\frac{xt^2 y + 1}{t^2} = 1 \\implies xt^2 y + 1 = t^2 \\implies xt^2 y = t^2 - 1 \\implies y = \\frac{t^2 - 1}{xt^2} = \\frac{t^2 - 1}{t^2 x}.\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[y = \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}}{x} = \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}}{1 + \\frac{1}{t}} = \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}}{1 + \\frac{1}{t}} = \\frac{\\frac{t^2 - 1}{t^2}}{1 + \\frac{1}{t}} = \\frac{\\frac{t^2 - 1}{t^2}}{\\frac{t + 1}{t}} = \\frac{t^2 - 1}{t^2 (t + 1)} = \\frac{(t - 1)(t + 1)}{t^3 (t + 1)} = \\frac{t - 1}{t^3}.\\]\n\nThis shows that the equation \\(xy = 1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}\\) is of the form \\(xy = 1 - \\frac{1}{t^2}\\), which is the standard form of a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a hyperbola.\nThe answer is: \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
